WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.400
Till now we have been looking at the properties of images

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.000
formed by plane mirrors.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:09.700
 

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:13.440
A plane mirror is a mirror whose reflecting surface is flat

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.700
What about this mirror?

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.260
Is this also a plane mirror?

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.800
This is a mirror as it has

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.740
the smooth reflecting surface.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.780
But this is not a plane mirror as this reflecting surface is not flat.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.500
The reflecting surface is curved and hence this mirror

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.160
is called a curved mirror.

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:35.245
What about this one?

00:00:35.595 --> 00:00:37.695
This is also a curved mirror

00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:41.585
The difference between the two is that reflecting surface

00:00:41.585 --> 00:00:44.180
of this mirror is curved inwards

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.640
where as the reflecting surface of this mirror is curved outwards.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.040
Can you think of examples

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.300
where you have seen curved mirrors?

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.280
A shiny surface of a spoon is a good approximation of curved mirrors.

00:00:56.875 --> 00:00:59.755
Spoon has two reflecting surfaces.

00:00:59.755 --> 00:01:03.255
One curved inside and the other curved outside.

00:01:03.395 --> 00:01:06.085
Now the specific types of curved mirrors that we

00:01:06.085 --> 00:01:09.235
are interested in are called Spherical mirrors.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.535
In fact, these two mirrors are spherical.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.000
Well, they look nothing like a sphere.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.880
But then why are they called so?

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.740
They are called spherical mirrors because  their reflecting surface

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.520
is a part of a sphere

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.160
You can clearly see that the reflecting surfaces

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.160
of both the mirrors form the part of the sphere.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.080
Now both these are given special names

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.680
The mirror that has it's reflecting surface curved inwards

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.340
is called a concave mirror

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.080
And the mirror that has it's reflecting surface curved outwards

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.020
is called convex mirror

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:43.900
Yes.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.020
Inwards - concave, and outwards - convex

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.020
Now we want to study the properties of images

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.980
formed by spherical mirrors.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.980
But before that, we need to define certain terms

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.760
related to spherical mirrors.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.200
So let's do that.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.440
Both the mirror are placed like this.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.360
Concave mirror on your left and convex mirror on your right.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.760
I'm going to define the terms for both these mirrors

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:14.880
Concave - inwards, and convex - outwards

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.020
The center of the reflecting surface of the mirror is called the pole

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.500
We represent it with a point P

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.480
In the front view of both the mirrors

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.260
You can't see that the pole is present right at the center

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.600
Now as I told you, the reflecting surface

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.540
forms a section of the sphere.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.100
This sphere must have a center

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.620
This center of the sphere is called center of curvature

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.120
denoted by C

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.540
And what will the radius be called?

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.620
Yes, it's called the radius of curvature

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.820
Join the pole and the center of curvature with a line

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.960
This line is called the Principal Axis

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.780
The midpoint of the line segment joining the pole P

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.100
and the center of curvature C

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.940
is called the focal point

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.080
It's denoted by the letter F

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.980
We will talk about one last thing in this video

00:03:08.645 --> 00:03:11.865
And that is an assumption we are making all along

00:03:12.285 --> 00:03:15.135
We are assuming the radius of the curvature of a mirror,

00:03:15.135 --> 00:03:17.875
to be must greater then the aperture of the mirror

00:03:19.125 --> 00:03:22.115
Aperture, in simple terms, is that part of the mirror

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.700
which is exposed to light.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:25.680
In our mirror

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.240
this is its aperture

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.480
since the light can be incident any where in this region.

00:03:31.275 --> 00:03:34.735
If we hide some part of the mirror with an opaque substance

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.040
then it's aperture is reduced.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.980
Mathematically it is this length.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.560
So the mirror that we are considering is the one in which the aperture

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.200
is much smaller then the radius of the curvature.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.160
These were a few of the terms related to spherical mirrors.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.380
But there's a lot more we need to understand about them.

00:03:53.665 --> 00:03:55.685
See you in the next video.

